[{:seon.task/name "an-interrupt-leaves-nothing-pending"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.intr`'s pending atom is set when root's interrupt finds no eval to stop and cleared only by `seon.intr/clear-pending!`, which the nREPL interrupt op (`seon.repl`) calls and root's `seon.system/interrupt!` (SIGUSR1) never sends; a set pending makes `seon.intr/run` start stopped. Reproduction (jolt/wiki 8c50215, through the server; re-verify first): after `seon.system/interrupt!` stopped `(loop [] (recur))` with \"interrupted by root\", every later eval in that agent bounded by :timeout-ms 200 (and in its clone) answered \"interrupted by root\" in 0.3 ms instead of running to its bound. Not traced: why pending was set although an eval was running when SIGUSR1 came. Correct: an interrupt ends exactly the form it reached; with no form running it is recorded as a no-op, and nothing carries into the next form or into a clone. Deletes the pending carry-over."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.intr-test/an-interrupt-ends-only-the-form-it-reached seon.intr-test/a-clone-starts-with-nothing-pending}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-interrupt-passes-every-catch"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Web, with Deep fixes for Jolt. Found 2026-11-12 by Web's sweep for guards. An interrupt is raised as (ex-info \"interrupted by root\" {:interrupted true}) (seon.intr), so every catch that must not swallow it rethrows it by hand: (when (:interrupted (ex-data e)) (throw e)) in seon.render's render! (twice) and seon.turn, and seon.repl branches on it. That is three hand guards, and {:interrupted true} is a bare key, against the owner's ruling that stored and public keys are namespaced. The ordinary way: on the JVM an interrupt is its own type (InterruptedException, or an Error that a catch of Exception does not take), so a catch passes it with no code. Plan: first read how Jolt raises an interrupt (:jolt/interrupted) and how Clojure's clojure.main and the JVM type it; raise it as one type that the edges' catches do not take; each edge catches what clojure.main's REPL catches less that type; delete the hand rethrows. Removes three guards and adds none. Mapping: needed as a thrown signal, because an interrupt stops code that is running and holds no channel to read; it is the JVM's Thread.interrupt, not a guard, and the work it ends reports its end on its channel as before. Proofs: seon.intr-test's interrupt tests unchanged and green, and a component render interrupted mid-walk passes the interrupt to the turn, not a component defect. Plan (Web, 2026-11-12): first read at a REPL how Jolt types :jolt/interrupted and whether a (catch C e) clause matches a host class (a question for deps if it does not). Then seon.intr raises one type: a :seon.intr/interrupted error schema, raised as an Error subclass Jolt names, if catch-by-class holds. The three hand rethrows (seon.render render! twice, seon.turn) and seon.repl's branch take (catch Exception e), which that type passes. Needs: deps' answer on catch-by-class. Estimate: seon.intr ~10 lines and 4 catch sites; the intr, render, turn and repl tests rerun. 90 min, plus deps."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.intr-test/an-interrupt-passes-a-component-and-reaches-the-turn}}]
